※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=64 diag=none

※ An answer that will not fit stops the program and says so.  It dies by a
※ status out of the range the runtime reserves for a stop it reports -- 64
※ through 127, with 64 the general one -- after writing what went wrong.  It
※ exits rather than dying by a signal: a shell reports a signal as 128 plus its
※ number, which collides with whatever a program might have chosen to exit
※ with, and a program that dies of a signal really did die of one.

let two_hundred: u8 = 200u8
let one_hundred: u8 = 100u8

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ which stops the program, so nothing below it runs
    if two_hundred + one_hundred ≠ 0u8:
        1u6
    else:
        0u6
